{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nEpic fattish-plucky-bufflehead (62dc-535b-5d05-421d) needs a retrieval architecture chosen on measurement, not on the July N = 8 experiment. The store holds a labeled set: every recorded `duplicates` link, closed transitively into clusters. That set has to be re-derived, because the numbers ticket exemplary-hexahedral-needlefish (eac3) measured are a July snapshot and no longer describe the store.\n\nRe-measured on tickets-branch 0b9294311b5 (5,663 live tickets): 482 recorded `duplicates` links close into 148 clusters over 630 tickets, enumerating to 1,524 within-cluster pairs. eac3 measured 2,679 tickets, 43 links and 51 pairs. The growth is not uniform, and the difference matters more than the count: **1,431 of the 1,524 pairs (94%) have byte-identical normalized titles**, concentrated in 84 clusters that are entirely one repeated title — the largest is 12 copies of \"A2-1: move last_get_pass rotation bookkeeping into .bridge_state/get_rotation.json\", next is 9 copies of \"Comment triage: relocate history, rationale and contracts out of src\". Those are bulk-creation artifacts, almost certainly a retry loop, not independently authored duplicates.\n\nThis is why the benchmark cannot simply be \"all recorded pairs\". Normalized-title matching scores ~100% recall on those 1,431, so a benchmark that includes them unstratified would report near-perfect recall for the cheapest lexical candidate and reject vector search on an artifact of bulk creation — inverting the epic's decision. eac3's central finding was the opposite: the duplicates worth catching are semantic, not lexical (it measured 14% lexical recall, 44 of 51 semantic).\n\n**93 pairs have differing titles.** Those are the semantic set, and they are the benchmark: double eac3's 44, same kind, drawn from 64 mixed clusters (56 of size 2). Samples confirm the character — \"Attested snapshot fetch pulls unrelated oversized tickets branch\" vs \"Attested ref materialization broad-fetches all origin heads\"; \"rebar doctor command\" vs \"rebar doctor: diagnose-and-heal health command\".\n\n## Approach\n\nBuild the fixture at a pinned tickets-branch SHA so the set is reproducible: collect every recorded `duplicates` link (`rebar list --full --exclude-deleted -o json`, reading each ticket's `deps` for the `duplicates` relation; the relation vocabulary is in `docs/ticket-model.md`), close transitively into clusters, enumerate within-cluster pairs, and **stratify each pair as `lexical` (normalized titles equal, case/punctuation/whitespace folded) or `semantic` (titles differ)**. Store pair ids, aliases, stratum, titles and the first 2,000 characters of description per ticket in `evals/similar-tickets/benchmark.json`, with the tickets-branch SHA and the per-stratum counts in a header object.\n\nReport recall@5 and recall@10 **per stratum, never pooled**, with the semantic stratum as the decision criterion and the lexical stratum as a control that every candidate is expected to near-saturate. Query text is title plus the first 200 characters of description (the compose-time shape). Candidates:\n\n1. Lexical baseline: SQLite FTS5 with `bm25()` over title (weight 5.0) and description (weight 1.0) in a temporary index built from the fixture.\n2. Digest lexical: BM25F over LLM digests produced with the retired `ticket_digest` prompt, read from commit **f147e08242f** (the parent of 2de5f341ec4, \"Remove the overlap/enrichment implementation\", which deleted it; the prompt lives in `src/rebar/llm/contracts.py` and `src/rebar/llm/overlap/` at that SHA). Trivial model class, one call per ticket, cached in the fixture directory keyed by content hash.\n3. Vector: embeddings of an LLM summary (the same digest text joined as one paragraph) from Amazon Titan Text Embeddings V2, model id `amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0`, called with the boto3 `bedrock-runtime` client's `invoke_model` and a JSON body of `{\"inputText\": ..., \"dimensions\": 1024, \"normalize\": true}` (input limit 8,192 tokens / 50,000 characters, so the summary is truncated to 40,000 characters before the call), region from `rebar.toml` `[llm] bedrock_region_name` (currently `us-east-1`, rebar.toml:213), boto3 reaching the venv through the `bedrock` extra's `pydantic-ai-slim[bedrock]`. Cosine similarity by brute force; 630 normalized vectors need no ANN library.\n\nWrite the ADR (next free number per `scripts/check_adr_numbers.py`) recording the per-stratum recall tables, the chosen architecture, the bulk-duplicate artifact and why the strata are reported separately, the index location (`<repo>/.git/rebar/similar.sqlite`, keyed by tickets-branch HEAD SHA plus a format version, rebuilt on mismatch), the catch-up rule (reader-process incremental update from `git diff --name-only <indexed_sha> <head>` on the tickets branch), and the cost model (summarization and embedding calls per changed ticket).\n\n## Scope\n\n- `evals/similar-tickets/benchmark.json` (new), `evals/similar-tickets/measure.py` (new; the runner for the three candidates), `evals/similar-tickets/README.md` (new). The top-level `evals/` tree already exists — it was created by commit 91d39fd6061, \"Move research corpus out of docs to evals/\", so the eval-harness dependency this story once waited on has landed.\n- `docs/adr/0NNN-similar-ticket-retrieval.md` (new).\n- `docs/adr/README.md` (regenerated by `scripts/gen_adr_index.py`).\n\n## Testing\n\n- Happy path: `python evals/similar-tickets/measure.py --candidate fts5` prints a per-stratum recall table; the fixture loads and every pair resolves to two existing ticket ids.\n- Edge: a pair whose ticket has an empty description still yields a query from the title alone; the runner skips a candidate cleanly when Bedrock credentials are absent and says so.\n- Edge: `--check` fails loudly if the fixture's recorded tickets-branch SHA is not an ancestor of the current tickets HEAD, so a stale fixture cannot be silently measured.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `evals/similar-tickets/benchmark.json` records the tickets-branch SHA it was derived at, and every pair carries ids, aliases, titles, description heads and a `lexical`/`semantic` stratum. `measure.py --check` resolves every id in the store and re-derives the pair set at that SHA, matching the fixture exactly. Checked by running the command.\n- [ ] Recall@5 and recall@10 are recorded **per stratum** for all three candidates in the ADR, produced by `measure.py` with the exact command lines quoted. No pooled figure is reported. Checked by re-running each command and matching the ADR tables.\n- [ ] The ADR names one architecture and justifies it from the **semantic** stratum, and records the index path, freshness key, catch-up rule, embedding model, per-ticket cost, and the bulk-duplicate artifact that makes stratification necessary. Checked by reading the ADR against `measure.py` output.\n- [ ] `scripts/gen_adr_index.py --check` and `scripts/check_adr_numbers.py` both exit 0."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793478570865111001,"uuid":"d31f99c9-4e8d-4061-aa48-57fbbdc80855"}